1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:06,367
At six thirty five in the morning, Universal Time, on August fifth, a piece of a rocket hit the Moon.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:09,310
Nobody launched it there on purpose.

3
00:00:09,310 --> 00:00:16,250
It was already in space, already dead, and it had been falling toward the Moon for over a year before anyone announced it.

4
00:00:17,399 --> 00:00:22,376
A spacecraft was two minutes away when it happened, cameras aimed at exactly the right spot.

5
00:00:22,376 --> 00:00:27,353
As of this recording, nobody outside that control room has seen what those cameras actually caught.

6
00:00:28,499 --> 00:00:29,910
You're watching mindwired.

7
00:00:31,033 --> 00:00:38,426
This is the story of how it got there, why it wasn't an accident that anyone meant, and why this isn't even the first time it's happened.

8
00:00:39,566 --> 00:00:44,817
The object that hit the Moon started its life eighteen months earlier, on a launch pad in Florida.

9
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:55,448
On January fifteenth, twenty twenty five, a Falcon nine rocket lifted off from Cape Canaveral, carrying Firefly Aerospace's Blue Ghost Mission One toward the Moon.

10
00:00:56,566 --> 00:00:59,544
Blue Ghost did exactly what it was built to do.

11
00:00:59,544 --> 00:01:02,522
It reached the Moon, and on March second, it landed.

12
00:01:03,666 --> 00:01:07,176
But the rocket that carried it there was never coming back.

13
00:01:07,176 --> 00:01:15,473
Once its job ended, the Falcon nine's upper stage was left exactly where it was, in a wide, unstable orbit around the Earth and Moon together.

14
00:01:16,599 --> 00:01:19,276
Nobody was flying it. Nobody was steering it.

15
00:01:19,276 --> 00:01:26,970
For the next year and a half, it simply drifted, and its orbit slowly, quietly bent toward a collision course with the Moon.

16
00:01:28,099 --> 00:01:30,650
Nobody at SpaceX announced any of this.

17
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:35,021
It was independent trackers, watching the sky, who worked it out first.

18
00:01:36,166 --> 00:01:44,995
Astronomers who track space debris as a hobby and a discipline noticed the object's orbit was decaying toward the Moon, and began refining exactly when and where it would hit.

19
00:01:46,133 --> 00:01:55,772
Their final prediction, updated just days before impact, put it at six thirty five in the morning, Universal Time, on August fifth, within a few seconds either way.

20
00:01:56,899 --> 00:02:04,866
The impact point: a spot near a crater called Einstein, right on the edge of the Moon's near side, the side we can actually see from Earth.

21
00:02:05,999 --> 00:02:13,470
Coming in at roughly five thousand four hundred miles an hour, a nearly five ton piece of metal, aimed at a target nobody chose.

22
00:02:14,599 --> 00:02:22,871
A predictable impact, with a known mass, a known speed, and a known time, is something that almost never happens in nature.

23
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:25,126
Scientists treated it as a gift.

24
00:02:26,266 --> 00:02:35,827
Professional and amateur astronomers across the Americas set up telescopes and high speed cameras, hoping to catch a flash, or a plume of dust rising off the lunar surface.

25
00:02:36,966 --> 00:02:39,840
But there was no guarantee anyone would see anything at all.

26
00:02:39,840 --> 00:02:48,199
The impact site sits right on the edge of what's visible from Earth, and whether it was even in view that morning depended on the Moon's own slow wobble in its orbit.

27
00:02:49,333 --> 00:02:55,496
The one spacecraft with a guaranteed view was South Korea's Danuri orbiter, which happened to be passing nearby.

28
00:02:55,496 --> 00:03:02,002
Mission controllers aimed its cameras to catch the impact from about two minutes away, the closest anyone would get.

29
00:03:03,133 --> 00:03:06,839
As of this recording, Danuri's images have not been made public.

30
00:03:06,839 --> 00:03:13,242
Three ground observatories did capture spectral data on the debris cloud, but that's a chemical signature, not a picture.

31
00:03:14,366 --> 00:03:18,507
NASA's own Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter photographed the impact site three weeks before it happened.

32
00:03:18,507 --> 00:03:22,647
It's scheduled to fly back over in the coming weeks to photograph what's left.

33
00:03:23,766 --> 00:03:27,594
Here's the part almost nobody watching the news this week seemed to know.

34
00:03:27,594 --> 00:03:31,128
This isn't remotely the first time something has crashed into the Moon.

35
00:03:31,128 --> 00:03:33,483
We've been doing it deliberately for sixty years.

36
00:03:34,633 --> 00:03:40,744
Between nineteen sixty four and nineteen sixty five, NASA sent a series of spacecraft called Ranger on a one way trip.

37
00:03:40,744 --> 00:03:47,146
Their entire mission was to fly straight at the Moon, taking pictures the whole way down, until the moment they hit it.

38
00:03:48,266 --> 00:03:55,345
This is real footage from Ranger nine, transmitted live in nineteen sixty five, right up until the signal simply stopped.

39
00:03:56,466 --> 00:04:09,475
Rangers seven, eight, and nine all successfully crashed on target, sending back the closest pictures of the lunar surface anyone had ever seen, each one a little sharper than the last, until there was nothing left to transmit.

40
00:04:10,599 --> 00:04:18,707
A few years later, during the Apollo missions, NASA started crashing rocket stages into the Moon on purpose again, for a completely different reason.

41
00:04:18,707 --> 00:04:20,395
They wanted to shake it.

42
00:04:21,533 --> 00:04:24,184
Apollo astronauts left seismometers on the lunar surface.

43
00:04:24,184 --> 00:04:34,124
By deliberately crashing spent rocket stages elsewhere on the Moon, mission control could measure how the shockwaves traveled, and learn what the inside of the Moon was actually made of.

44
00:04:35,266 --> 00:04:39,591
One honest note. No camera was ever pointed at any of those Apollo impacts.

45
00:04:39,591 --> 00:04:47,622
What you sometimes see labeled as footage of one is a NASA reconstruction, built afterward from the seismometer data, not a real recording of the moment.

46
00:04:48,766 --> 00:04:54,798
In two thousand nine, NASA did it once more, on purpose, with a mission called LCROSS.

47
00:04:54,798 --> 00:05:02,715
They aimed a spent rocket stage at a permanently shadowed crater called Cabeus, specifically hoping the impact would kick up ice.

48
00:05:03,833 --> 00:05:06,493
It worked. The debris plume contained water.

49
00:05:06,493 --> 00:05:12,192
It was the first direct confirmation that the Moon wasn't as dry as everyone assumed.

50
00:05:13,333 --> 00:05:20,412
Which brings us to the impact that makes this week's crash genuinely strange, and not just another rocket falling out of the sky.

51
00:05:21,533 --> 00:05:28,362
On March fourth, twenty twenty two, another dead rocket stage hit the Moon's far side, near a crater called Hertzsprung.

52
00:05:28,362 --> 00:05:31,094
Nobody had announced that one in advance either.

53
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:39,575
When NASA's orbiter photographed the site afterward, it found something engineers hadn't seen from a single impact before.

54
00:05:39,575 --> 00:05:42,839
Not one crater. Two, overlapping, side by side.

55
00:05:43,966 --> 00:05:51,710
The leading explanation is that the rocket body had weight on both ends, unevenly, and struck the ground twice within a fraction of a second.

56
00:05:51,710 --> 00:05:54,807
But that raised a bigger question. Whose rocket was it?

57
00:05:55,933 --> 00:06:04,919
Independent trackers, working backward from its orbit, identified it as the third stage of China's Chang'e five T one mission, launched back in twenty fourteen.

58
00:06:06,066 --> 00:06:09,598
China's government disputed that identification when asked about it directly.

59
00:06:09,598 --> 00:06:14,895
To this day, no government has officially claimed the object that made that double crater.

60
00:06:16,033 --> 00:06:20,758
So that's where this week's impact actually sits. Not the first deliberate crash.

61
00:06:20,758 --> 00:06:22,939
Not even the first accidental one.

62
00:06:22,939 --> 00:06:33,117
Just the newest entry in a list that started with Ranger, continued through Apollo and LCROSS, and now includes at least two unclaimed rocket bodies, six years apart.

63
00:06:34,266 --> 00:06:41,070
As of this recording, the pictures from this week's impact are still sitting in a camera on a spacecraft that was two minutes away.

64
00:06:41,070 --> 00:06:43,905
NASA's own before and after photographs are still weeks off.

65
00:06:45,033 --> 00:06:48,699
By the time those pictures come out, we'll already be looking at the next one.

66
00:06:48,699 --> 00:06:56,031
If this is the kind of story where the answer arrives after the cameras stop rolling, wait until you see what's still unexplained about the crater from twenty twenty two.

67
00:06:57,166 --> 00:06:58,166
This is mindwired.

68
00:06:58,166 --> 00:07:04,167
Subscribe, and when the real pictures of this impact finally do come out, you'll see them here first.

